{
  "fromId": "x",
  "toId": "q",
  "preamble": "The voiceless velar fricative [x] replaced by the uvular stop [q] in loanword adaptation in Turkic languages that lack native /x/. Kazakh, Kyrgyz and Shor rendered the [x] of Arabic and Persian back-vowel loans with /q/, the back-vowel member of the native k/q pair, while Uzbek, Azerbaijani and Turkmen keep a fricative or [h]. In Kazakh the fricative has since been restored in the standard language, but nativized pronunciations with [q] persist.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fortition, Stopping",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Kazakh",
      "languageFamily": "Kipchak",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Arabic خَزِينَة xazīna 'treasury'",
          "to": "қазына qazına 'treasure, treasury'",
          "note": "cf. Uzbek xazina, Azerbaijani xəzinə, which keep the fricative."
        },
        {
          "from": "Classical Persian خواجه xwāja 'master'",
          "to": "қожа qoja 'hodja, master'"
        },
        {
          "from": "халық /χɑ.ləq/ 'people' (< Persian xalq)",
          "to": "nativized [qɑ.ɫəq]",
          "note": "Standard Kazakh has reintroduced the fricative in this loan, but the nativized pronunciation with initial [q] persists."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Kyrgyz",
      "languageFamily": "Kipchak",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Classical Persian xalq (< Arabic ḵalq) 'people'",
          "to": "калк /qɑlq/ 'population'"
        },
        {
          "from": "Arabic خَبَر xabar 'news'",
          "to": "кабар /qaˈβar/ 'news'",
          "note": "Kazakh хабар and Uzbek xabar keep the fricative."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Shor",
      "languageFamily": "Siberian Turkic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Arabic خَبَر xabar 'news'",
          "to": "қабар qabar 'news'"
        },
        {
          "from": "Classical Persian xalq 'people'",
          "to": "қалық qalïq 'people'",
          "note": "Listed among the cognates of Kazakh халық and Kyrgyz калк."
        }
